<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
    <string>VisualTalk needs access to your camera for video calls.</string>
    <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
    <string>VisualTalk needs access to your microphone for audio communication.</string>

    <!--
      NOTE: tauri.conf.json points BOTH bundle.macOS.infoPlist and
      bundle.iOS.infoPlist at this file, so everything here is merged into the
      macOS bundle too. The keys below are marked where they are iOS-only;
      macOS ignores keys it does not recognise, so one shared file is safer than
      a second merge path nothing currently verifies.

      Tauri also auto-discovers an `Info.ios.plist` beside this file for
      iOS-only keys, but that is not pinned by config here, so we do not rely
      on it.
    -->

    <!-- Display name. Tauri derives this from `productName`, which is the
         lowercase "visualtalk"; without this override the App Store listing and
         the iOS home screen both render it uncapitalised. -->
    <key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
    <string>VisualTalk</string>

    <!-- iOS only; inert on macOS. Keeps the meeting audio session alive when the
         app is backgrounded or the screen locks — without it iOS suspends the
         socket and audio drops mid-call. `audio` is the right mode for LiveKit;
         `voip` would additionally require PushKit and invites extra review. -->
    <key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
    <array>
        <string>audio</string>
    </array>

    <!-- iOS 14+ requires this string before opening any local-network
         connection. The AI backend defaults to http://localhost:11434 and is
         user-configurable to a LAN address, so the prompt can legitimately
         fire. -->
    <key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
    <string>VisualTalk connects to an AI service on your local network to power live captions and summaries.</string>

    <!-- EXPORT COMPLIANCE
         `ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption = NO` means "this app uses no encryption
         that is *non-exempt*" — i.e. either none at all, or only cryptography
         that qualifies for an exemption / license exception. It does NOT claim
         the app uses no cryptography. Setting it here makes App Store Connect
         stop asking the export-compliance question on every submission.

         VisualTalk's complete cryptographic inventory:

           1. TLS on wss:// to the LiveKit server (rustls → aws-lc-rs/ring).
           2. DTLS-SRTP for media, implemented by the WebRTC/LiveKit stack.
           3. AES-GCM end-to-end encryption of chat and file transfers (Pro).
           4. HMAC-SHA256 over the locally-minted connection JWT (token.rs) —
              authentication/integrity only, no confidentiality.
           5. HTTPS to a user-configured AI endpoint.

         All of it uses standard, publicly documented algorithms (TLS, SRTP,
         AES, HMAC-SHA2) rather than any proprietary or custom cipher, and the
         source is publicly available. That places it under the mass-market /
         publicly-available provisions — the Note to Category 5 Part 2 and
         License Exception ENC (15 CFR 740.17) — which is the exemption Apple's
         "non-exempt" wording refers to. Hence NO.

         TWO CAVEATS BEFORE YOU SHIP:

         a) This is a legal determination, not an engineering one. The reasoning
            above is the standard position for a video-calling app and is the
            one Apple's own guidance points at, but confirm it with counsel —
            especially item 3, since E2EE of user content is confidentiality of
            data rather than transport-only protection, which is the one place a
            stricter reading could differ.

         b) If you rely on License Exception ENC, BIS requires an annual
            self-classification report (due 1 February each year, to
            crypt@bis.doc.gov and ENC Regs Self-classification Reporting). That
            obligation attaches to the exporter — Miradata — not to Apple, and
            filing it is easy to overlook. Confirm whether it applies to you.

         If counsel concludes the app does use non-exempt encryption, set this
         to <true/> and answer the questionnaire in App Store Connect instead. -->
    <key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
    <false/>

    <!-- iOS only; inert on macOS. Exposes the app's Documents directory in the
         Files app ("On My iPhone → VisualTalk") and lets documents be opened in
         place. Local recordings are written to Documents/Recordings on iOS
         precisely so this makes them reachable — without these two keys the
         sandbox has no file manager and a recording can never be retrieved,
         which is the mobile half of the "where did my recording go" problem
         that Settings → Recordings solves on desktop. -->
    <key>UIFileSharingEnabled</key>
    <true/>
    <key>LSSupportsOpeningDocumentsInPlace</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>